# ~/.aida/op.env: an alternative to ~/.aida/.env for machines that pull
# secrets from 1Password instead of typing real keys into a plain-text
# file. Loaded after .env (internal/config/config.go's LoadDotEnv), so a
# key set in .env still wins; op.env is meant for tokens .env doesn't
# have yet. Never rendered by any secrets-sync tooling and never
# committed -- this .example file is the only op.env-shaped thing that
# belongs in git.
#
# OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN authenticates the `op` CLI as a 1Password
# service account (headless, no interactive sign-in) -- see
# https://developer.1password.com/docs/service-accounts/ for how to
# create one and https://developer.1password.com/docs/cli/ for the CLI
# itself. A typical setup is a small script that runs
# `op inject --force` against a template to populate real values, or
# `op read` for one-off lookups; either way, the resulting real
# secrets belong in ~/.aida/op.env (or ~/.aida/.env), never here.
OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN=ops_eyJEXAMPLE-not-a-real-token

# Interim per-session tokens some 1Password workflows export can also be
# placed here as plain `export`-style lines; config.go strips a leading
# `export ` before parsing each line the same way it parses this file's
# KEY=value lines above.
# export OP_SESSION_my_account=EXAMPLE-not-a-real-session-token
